---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';

const title = 'Home';
const description =
  'Build Android APK and AAB files from Command Prompt or PowerShell without Android Studio. A self-adapting pipeline that finds the JDK, sets up the SDK, and signs the release for you.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} wide>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>Build Android APKs without Android Studio</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Android APK Builder compiles Kotlin, Java, and Jetpack Compose projects
      straight from Command Prompt or PowerShell on Windows 10 and 11.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    The tool is a single PowerShell engine (plus a <code>build.bat</code> launcher) that
    inspects your project, picks the right JDK and Gradle version, prepares the Android
    SDK, compiles, signs, and copies the artifacts into <code>dist</code>. Android Studio
    idles at 4 to 8 GB of RAM before you build anything; this engine runs in a terminal
    and doses memory per hardware profile instead.
  </p>

  <h2>What the engine does automatically</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Reads Groovy, Kotlin DSL, and version catalog build files to learn the SDK,
      build-tools, and AGP version of the project.
    </li>
    <li>Resolves JDK, Gradle, and AGP against an integrated compatibility matrix.</li>
    <li>
      Profiles host RAM and applies matching JVM heap ceilings, GC choice, and worker
      limits, so 4 GB laptops still build reliably.
    </li>
    <li>Installs missing JDKs and SDK packages, recovering broken Gradle wrappers.</li>
    <li>
      Signs release builds, generates a keystore when none exists, and verifies the
      result with SHA-256 hashes plus apksigner v1 through v4 checks.
    </li>
    <li>
      Keeps the host clean: environment variables are scoped to the session, and
      artifacts are copied, never moved.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Try it in two commands</h2>
  <CodeBlock
    caption="Clone"
    code={`git clone https://github.com/tanvirr007/android-apk-builder.git`}
  />
  <CodeBlock
    caption="Open directory"
    code={`cd android-apk-builder`}
  />

  <p>
    From there, simply double-click on the <code>build.bat</code> file or right-click and choose <strong>Run as administrator</strong> if needed.
    The interactive wizard launches and asks for your Android project path. Simply type or paste the path, or drag and drop your project folder directly into the window to start building.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <a href="/how-to-use">How to Use</a> page explains every mode,
    command-line invocation, release signing, and hardware profiling in detail.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
